== Getting Married

=== §. 14. #emph[Of a contract: what it is.]

#smallcaps[II]. When both parties have shown a mutual liking #footnote[#emph[Ubi non est consensus vtriusque, non potest esse matrimonium. Instit. iur. can. lib.] 2. #emph[de nupt. tit.] 11. \[Where there is not the consent of both, there can be no marriage.\]] for each other, and after mature deliberation and good advice judge each other to be a fit match, it is needful that a joint consent and absolute promise to marry one another be made before sufficient witnesses. This, rightly made, is a #emph[contract,] which is the beginning of a marriage.

The right making of a firm contract consists in two things:

1\. In an actual taking of each other as betrothed man and wife.

2\. In a direct promise to marry each other within a fitting time. So a form of contract may be made to this effect. First the man, taking the woman by the hand, says, #emph[I, A., take you, B., to be my betrothed wife, and faithfully promise to marry you at a fit and proper time.] And then the woman, in turn taking the man by the hand, says, #emph[I, B., take you, A., to be my betrothed husband, and faithfully promise to consent to be married to you at a fit and proper time.] This mutual and actual taking of one another as betrothed man and wife in the present time, together with a direct promise to marry one another afterward, settles such a right and ownership of the one in the other as cannot be given away without leave from the great Judge of heaven, who by his divine ordinance has settled that right.

=== §. 15. #emph[Of the grounds of a contract.]

#emph[Question.] Is a contract absolutely necessary?

#emph[Answer.] Though it has been an ancient custom, continued #footnote[#emph[Mos ille veteribus erat ante multum tempus sponsalia facere. Chrys. in Gen. Hom.] 43. \[It was the custom of the ancients to make the betrothal a long time beforehand.\]] in all ages, yet I dare not pronounce it so absolutely necessary that the lack of it should nullify a marriage and make it no lawful marriage. But surely it is so fitting and needful that I would advise all Christians who desire a blessing and good success on their marriage to make a contract before they are married.

If there were no other ground for it but this, that God has sanctified it and commended it to us by his word, it would be enough to persuade those who fear God to use it.

For I ask, why does God commend anything to his church by his word, except that his church should make conscience of using it? Now that it is commended by God’s word is most evident, beyond contradiction. For I will not insist on the argument which, not without good probability, is brought for the antiquity of a contract, gathered from God’s ordering of that first ancient marriage between Adam and #footnote[Gen 2:22.] Eve. God brought Eve to Adam on the first day that they were created, to see how he would like her; and on seeing her, and on the knowledge of her that he gained at the same time by the wisdom God gave him, he then took her to himself. This taking is supposed to have been only a contract, because #footnote[Gen 4:1.] he did not know her until after his fall. If it is objected that Adam and Eve are called #emph[man]#footnote[Gen 2:25.] and #emph[wife] in the time of their innocence, it may be answered that those titles #footnote[Deut 22:24. Gen 19:14. #emph[Consuetudine Scripturarum sponsi viri, & sponsae vocantur vxores. Hier. in Mat.] 1.] are given in Scripture to those who are only betrothed, to show the close and firm union between betrothed parties.

But not to insist on what is only probable, it is more than probable that Lot’s daughters were betrothed. For it is said that Lot#footnote[Gen 19:14, 8. #emph[Quia iam desponsatio facta erat, ideo vocat eos generos. Chrys. in hunc loc.]] had “sons-in-law who married” (or rather were to marry) “his daughters,” and it is also said that they “have never known a man.” It must follow, then, that they were called Lot’s sons-in-law because his daughters were betrothed to them. But most evident for this purpose is the difference which the #footnote[Deut 22:22, #emph[etc.]] law puts between a pure virgin, a betrothed virgin and a married wife. So betrothed persons are in a middle degree between single persons and married persons: they are neither wholly single nor actually married. To show that this custom of a contract before marriage continued in God’s church among the saints, it is expressly noted that Mary, the mother of the Lord #footnote[Luke 1:27.] Jesus, who lived many hundreds of years after that law, was betrothed.

=== §. 16. #emph[Of the reasons which show how needful a contract is.]

Many good and weighty reasons may be brought to show how needful it is that a contract should go before marriage. For

1\. It adds much to the honor of marriage that it should be completed and made up deliberately and with good advice, step after step, by one degree after another.

2\. It puts a difference between those who intend marriage in the fear of the Lord, for such holy ends as are warranted in the word, and those who intend it only to satisfy their lust, or for other such carnal ends. For #emph[these] can admit no delay, as was noted #footnote[In the end of §. 13.] before. But #emph[those] who use this solemn preparation of a contract before marriage show that they desire to have everything fitting for so sacred a matter duly performed; and therefore they are content to accept some delay, so that it may be the better accomplished.

3\. It is a means of knitting the hearts of the two parties who are to be married more firmly and inviolably together before they come to live together. For since a contract is the beginning of a marriage, it is an evident demonstration of God’s counsel concerning the betrothed parties, that God has prepared them for each other to be man and wife. So after the contract is made, they may pray for each other absolutely and without condition, that God would bless them to one another, to live together in comfort and happiness. Before a contract is made, they can pray for one another only on a supposition (if God has appointed them to be man and wife). For it often happens that after many great hopes and likelihoods of going forward with this or that match, it is broken off completely by some occasion or other. But a lawful contract ties so firm a knot that it cannot be broken, so that a man who is betrothed to a woman may conclude that she will be his wife, and a woman may conclude the same of a man. Considering this will further move them to observe more closely what good qualities, or what other things that may make them more lovely to one another, are in each other.

4\. It is a means to make them prepare themselves beforehand to perform such marriage duties as God’s word requires of man and wife. For a contract gives them assurance of marriage if they live to see it. So they cannot help knowing that it is high time for them to think how they are to conduct themselves when they come to live in a house together, and also to consider what crosses ordinarily accompany the married state, so that they may be prepared beforehand wisely to pass through them or patiently to bear them.

5\. It may be a means of revealing many hidden and secret hindrances which otherwise would never come to light. For many friends, fearing that revealing some evils which are kept secret should make a breach between themselves and their friends, will not make them known until they see some urgent necessity moving them to do so. Though the evil is such that, if it were known, it would hinder the marriage, yet until they see some sure evidence that the couple will indeed be married (if no just objection is raised), they will hope that some other occasion may arise to hinder the marriage, and for that reason they conceal their objection. But because a contract is the beginning of marriage, once they have notice of it they will not hold back from disclosing what they know. It is for this end that the contract is openly published in the church three separate times, so that if anyone knows any just cause why such betrothed persons may not lawfully proceed to marriage, they may make it known. It is a commendable custom, and a great pity that it is so much neglected as it is.

6\. It may prevent many plots and schemes of enticing or stealing away young women and widows. For it often happens that when parents or other relatives have provided a good match for their daughter, or for someone else under their government, and everything has been well settled on all sides, the wedding day appointed, and all things fitted and prepared for solemnizing the wedding, some who want to forestall that marriage get the bride away by secret and cunning devices a few days before, if not on the very morning of the intended wedding day, and marry her at once to another man. What makes men so bold is that they know that a clandestine marriage, once completed, will stand firm in law. But a legal contract prevents such injuries, because it makes such a furtive marriage utterly void. No one, therefore, who knows that a contract has been lawfully made beforehand will be so bold, or rather so mad, as to attempt to frustrate a marriage in any such way.

7\. It is a means to stir up the parties who are to be married to provide beforehand, more carefully and diligently, all things fitting for their living together and for the good ordering of their household, so that they are not left looking for necessities when they ought to be using them. For being betrothed, they know that it cannot be long before they must come to live together.

=== §. 17. #emph[Of abusing, or neglecting a contract.]

There are two extremes contrary to the doctrine of a contract set out above: one of attributing too much to it, the other of detracting too much from it. Many make it a marriage in itself, and so have a greater solemnity at their contract than #footnote[Nonnullis sponsis tam impotens est ardor libidinis, vt corpusculo puellari vim adferant. Eras. in Inst. Matr. \[In some betrothed men the heat of lust is so ungoverned that they do violence to the young girl’s body.\]] at their marriage. Yes, many take the liberty after a contract to know their spouse as if they were married: an unwarranted and unchaste practice. Lot’s daughters were #footnote[See §. 15.] betrothed to husbands, and yet it is said that they “have never known a man.” The law calls her who is betrothed “a virgin,”#footnote[Deut 22:24.] to show that she ought to keep herself a virgin until the marriage is completed. Therefore Mary is described this way: “a virgin pledged in marriage.”#footnote[Luke 1:27.] But it is the common course of most people to make light of this warranted and honorable way of proceeding to marriage by a contract. There are few, compared with the multitudes who are married, who make any conscience of it. They think it needless and utterly neglect it. No wonder they meet with many injuries and troubles, when the means of preventing them are not used. Let such people duly weigh the reasons given in the previous section.

=== §. 18. #emph[Of the length of time between the contract and marriage.]

#emph[Question.] What length of time must pass between the making of a contract and the completing of the marriage?

#emph[Answer.] This must be left to the wise consideration of the betrothed parties and of their friends, for the same time cannot be precisely prescribed for all. Occasions may arise either for hastening or for putting off the marriage. Only the extremes on both sides must be avoided. The marriage ought neither to be solemnized too suddenly after the contract (then the ends and reasons of a contract, mentioned before, are made void), nor yet to be put off too long (then Satan may take occasion to tempt them through their lack of self-control). The commendable custom of our church and other churches shows that at least three weeks must pass between contract and marriage. For the contract is #footnote[#emph[Rubr.] before the form of solemnizing of matrimony.] to be published three times, and that only once a week, before the wedding is celebrated. And we read that the virgin Mary was betrothed for at least three months before Joseph took her as his wife. For when the angel first came to her she was #footnote[Luke 1:27, 56.] betrothed; after that she went to her relative Elizabeth, with whom she stayed three months; and then, when she had returned home, Joseph was warned by an angel to take her as his wife.#footnote[Matt 1:20.] I do not note this as a rule for everyone to follow precisely. For the virgin Mary had a just occasion to stay three months with her aged relative Elizabeth, and so others may have occasions to put off their marriages. This may be lawful, provided the marriage is not put off too long, and that there is a mutual and joint consent of both parties. For after the contract is made, neither the man nor the woman has power over their own body.

Contrary to this is the unwarranted course which many take, to #footnote[Unlawful for betrothed parties to be long absent from each other. Deut 24:5.] be betrothed and pledged to a wife, and then to travel overseas, or to any other place, and be absent from their spouse a year, or two, or three, or perhaps more years. If a man was not allowed to go to war, nor to be charged with any business that would draw him away from his wife, in the first year of his marriage, much less may he absent himself for any long time after he is betrothed but not married. This may be a means of turning the heart of his spouse away from him forever.

=== §. 19. #emph[Of a religious consecrating of marriage.]

#smallcaps[III]. The last degree in completing a marriage is its open and public #emph[solemnization,] which consists in a religious #emph[consecration] and a civil #emph[celebration] of it.

A religious #emph[consecration] of marriage is performed by the blessing of a public minister of the word, openly before the church, in the daytime. This has been the practice of Christians from of old, and it is still continued among us. Though we have neither an express precept nor a particular pattern in God’s word for this manner of solemnizing a marriage (for no particular form of it is set down in the Scripture), yet since it agrees with the general rules of Scripture, we ought in conscience to subject ourselves to it.

The general rules are these: “Everything must be done decently #footnote[1 Cor 14:40.] and in order”; the churches of God have such a custom; “Submit #footnote[1 Cor 11:16.] yourselves#super[+] for the Lord’s sake to every human ordinance,”#footnote[1 Pet 2:13.] and the like. But the manner of consecrating marriage described above is very decent and in good order, a commendable custom of the churches, and an ordinance of the rulers under whom we live. Therefore it is warranted, and to be observed. Nor is this order, custom and ordinance appointed without just and weighty reasons. For

1\. Marriage is a kind of public action: its good or bad ordering tends greatly to the good or harm of family, church and commonwealth. For by marriage families are set up, and church and commonwealth are increased and continued.

2\. “Marriage is honorable.”#footnote[Heb 13:4.] #footnote[Treatise 1, §. 80, 81, 82, #emph[etc.]] It is the most sacred and inviolable bond that knits any two persons together.

3\. Marriage is #footnote[Prov 2:17.] God’s covenant, in which he himself has a main and principal hand. For God is the chief agent in joining man and woman in marriage.

On these grounds it has been thought very fitting that marriage should be solemnized in the daytime (as a work of light that need not be done covertly and secretly), and in a public place, where anyone who wishes may have free access, either to object to it and hinder it (if there is just cause) or to be a witness of it and add his blessing to it. And among public places the church is thought the fittest, because it is the house of prayer, where persons and actions are most solemnly blessed. And of all persons a public minister is thought the most fitting to celebrate marriage and to join #footnote[#emph[Coniugium benedictione sacerdotali sanctificari oportet. Ambr. epist.] 70. \[Marriage ought to be sanctified by the priest’s blessing.\]] the parties to be married together, because he stands in God’s place, and in and by his ministry God joins them together and blesses them. So after the minister has rightly joined man and wife together in marriage, it may well be said, “What God has joined together, let man not separate.” The form of consecrating marriage which is prescribed in our liturgy, or Book of Common Prayer, sets down so distinctly, clearly and fully whatever is to be observed and done by the parties to be married, their parents or other guardians, and the minister who joins them together, that I can add nothing to it. There the grounds, ends and uses of marriage are declared. There open proclamation is made whether anyone can object to the intended marriage. There each party is solemnly charged, if either of them knows any impediment why they may not lawfully be married, to disclose it. There also each party is openly asked whether they will freely and willingly take one another as man and wife. There the duties of married persons are declared, and they are each asked whether they will subject themselves to them or not. When all this has been openly professed, the parent, or someone in his place, is called forward to give the bride to the bridegroom. Then, when the two of them have actually taken each other as man and wife, and testified to it by express words and by mutual pledges, the minister joins them together in God’s name, pronounces them to be lawful husband and wife, and by prayer asks God’s blessing on the action and on their persons. In this way the marriage is consecrated, and the two of them are made one flesh, that is, lawfully joined together by the inviolable bond of marriage.

=== §. 20. #emph[Of clandestine marriages.]

Contrary to this are clandestine marriages, such as are made in private houses or other secret places, or in churches without a sufficient number of witnesses, or in the nighttime, or without a lawful minister of the word, and the like. As such seeking of secrecy takes much away from the honor and dignity of marriage, so it implies some evil clinging to it: for “everyone #footnote[John 3:20.] who does evil hates the light.” There is little hope that such marriages should have any good success. For where such means as are sanctified for obtaining a blessing on marriage are neglected, what blessing can be expected from it?

=== §. 21. #emph[Of a civil celebrating of marriage.]

Though by the consecrating of marriage named above it is fully complete in its substance, yet for the greater solemnity of so honorable a thing it is very fitting that a #emph[civil celebration] of it should be added as well, under #footnote[#emph[Magnifico conuiuio nuptiae celebrantur: laeta omnia, munera sponsalia, etc. Greg. Naz. ad Vital. vide plura ibid. de coniugij solennitate.] \[A wedding is celebrated with a splendid feast: everything is joyful, there are betrothal gifts, etc. See more in the same place on the solemnity of marriage.\] Marriage a time of rejoicing.] which I include all the lawful customs that are used to set forth its outward solemnity, such as the meeting of friends, accompanying the bridegroom and bride both to and from the church, putting on one’s best clothing, feasting, and other tokens of rejoicing. For these we have express warrant from God’s word.

As to the general point, that the time of marriage is a time of rejoicing, some gather it from the derivation of the word, as if it were called #emph[marriage] from #emph[merry age.] But to let that pass, the Scripture is accustomed to set forth a time of rejoicing by the joy of the bridegroom, “as a bridegroom rejoices over his bride,”#footnote[Isa 62:5.] and it speaks of “the sounds of joy and gladness, the voices of the bride and bridegroom.”#footnote[Jer 33:11.] And on the other hand it counts it a judgment when #footnote[Jer 7:34. Joel 2:16.] joy is taken away from the bridegroom and the bride.

As for the meeting of friends at the time of marriage, it is noted that “Laban invited all the men of that place”#footnote[Gen 29:22.] when his daughter was married. And when Samson was married, “they selected thirty men to accompany him.”#footnote[Judg 14:11.] And when a friend of the virgin Mary was married, #footnote[John 2:2.] Jesus and his disciples, besides many others, were invited to it. And in the parable of the wedding of the king’s son, it is noted that many guests were invited to the wedding.#footnote[Matt 22:3.] In all these histories it is further noted that feasts were made at the solemnizing of those marriages. And the words the prophet uses, “as a bridegroom adorns himself with ornaments, as a bride adorns herself with her jewels,”#footnote[Isa 61:10. Jer 2:32.] give warrant for putting on the best clothing at that time.

Here, by the way, let good heed be taken that the things which may lawfully be used are not unlawfully abused, as marriage festivities commonly are, and especially in feasting.

=== §. 22. #emph[Of ill or well ordering marriage feasts.]

Marriage feasts are abused, #footnote[Abuse of marriage feasts.]

1\. When they are held at an unseasonable time:#footnote[Isa 22:12, 13.] such as on the Lord’s day, or in a time of mourning.

2\. When they exceed the means of him who makes the #footnote[Luke 15:13.] feasts.

3\. When the abundance prepared is taken immoderately, #footnote[Luke 21:34.] even to gluttony and drunkenness. The wicked practice of drinking toasts to the bridegroom and bride often causes much excess in drunkenness.

4\. When too much time is spent on them. #footnote[Isa 5:11.]

5\. When God is completely forgotten in them, and the company is poisoned #footnote[Isa 5:12.] with corrupt talk, unchaste songs and the like.

6\. When the needy and distressed are not remembered #footnote[Amos 6:6.] at them.

To prevent these abuses, a seasonable time for celebrating #footnote[Direction for marriage feasts] marriage must be chosen, and moderation used both by him who makes the feast and also by those who share in it: moderation, I say, in the measure of eating and drinking, and in the time spent on it. That time must be sanctified with such talk as is “helpful for building up” and “bringing grace to those who listen.” And for more cheerfulness in it, clever questions and puzzling riddles may be posed (as Samson did)#footnote[Judg 14:12. Eph 5:19.] to exercise the understanding and judgment of the guests. There may also be the singing of “psalms, hymns, and spiritual songs,” the guests making music in their hearts to the Lord. Yes, further, so that the marriage meeting, mirth and feasting may be the better sanctified, a good choice is to be made of the guests who are invited to it. Of this we have a worthy pattern in those who were married in Cana of Galilee, #footnote[John 2:1, 2.] and invited Jesus, his mother and his disciples to their feast. Guests such as Jesus will give occasion for wholesome and sanctified talk, by which all the guests may be much edified. Such as the virgin Mary will be a good example of modesty, sobriety and other such Christian graces. Such as the disciples will be far from scorning and mocking sound and good instruction, but will rather give all diligent heed to it, and lay it up in their hearts.

Above all, so that the mirth and joy of marriage is not dampened as Belshazzar’s was,#footnote[Dan 5:5.] let those who are married, and all who come to rejoice with them, make sure that they have true assurance of their spiritual marriage with Christ, and of a good right in him to the created things which they use. Otherwise their sins will be like that handwriting which appeared to Belshazzar on the wall.

Finally, because of the liberty which God gives, with plenty and abundance, to eat what is rich and drink what is #footnote[Neh 8:10.] sweet, let the poor who scarcely have enough be remembered.

So by a right celebrating of marriage it is much honored, and man and wife are brought together with much honor.

=== §. 23. #emph[Of the honor of marriage with respect to its first institution.]

There is great reason why marriage should be solemnized with such honor. For it is a most honorable thing: honorable in its institution, ends, privileges and mystery. No ordinance was more honorable in its first institution, as is evident from its #emph[Author,] the #emph[place] where it was instituted, the #emph[time] when it was instituted, the #emph[persons] who were first married, and the #emph[manner] of joining them together.

1\. The #emph[Author] and first founder of marriage was the Lord #footnote[Gen 2:18, 21, 22] God. Could there have been a greater, or in any way a more excellent, Author?

2\. The #emph[place] was Paradise, the most fair, glorious, pleasant, honorable, well-provided and in every way most excellent place that ever was in this world. Place, though it is only a circumstance, adds much to the honor of a thing. Solemn ordinances are made in honorable places. So with us marriages are solemnized in churches, not in private houses.

3\. The #emph[time] was the most pure and perfect time that ever was in the world, when no sin or pollution of man had stained it: the time of man’s innocence. Purity adds much to the honor of a thing.

4\. The #emph[persons] were the most honorable that ever were: the first father and mother of all mankind, those who had absolute power and dominion over all creatures, and to whom all were subject. No one but they ever had a true monarchy over the whole world.

5\. The #emph[manner] was with as great deliberation as was ever used in instituting any ordinance. For first, the three glorious #footnote[Gen 2:18. #emph[Cui hoc dicitur? non creatae alicui potentiae, sed ei qui ex se natus est, Consiliario admirabili, etc. Chrys. in Gen. hom.] 14.] persons in the Trinity meet to consult about it. For “the #smallcaps[Lord] God also said,” and to whom should he speak? Not to any created power, but to him who was begotten of himself, the “Wonderful Counselor,” etc. In this consultation this ordinance is found to be very needful (“It is not good for the man to be alone”), and so a decision is set down, to make “a suitable helper” for man. To carry this out the better, the Lord proceeds to it very deliberately, by various steps and degrees.

#[
#set enum(indent: 24pt)
+ All the creatures that lived on the earth or breathed in the air are brought before man, to see if a suitable helper for him might be found among them.
+ Each of them being thoroughly viewed and found unfit, another creature is made, and that out of man’s substance and side, and after his image.
+ This excellent creature, so made, is presented to man by its maker, to see how he would like it.
+ Man showing a good liking for her, she is given to him to be his wife.
+ The inviolable law of the close and firm union of man and wife together is enacted.
]

Let all the branches named above concerning the first institution of marriage, expressly recorded by the Holy Spirit, be well weighed, and we will easily see that there is no ordinance now in force among the sons of men so honorable in its institution as this.

=== §. 24. #emph[Of the Ends of Marriage.]

2\. The #emph[ends] for which marriage was ordained add much to its honor. They are chiefly three. #footnote[#emph[Prima societas in ipso coniugio est etc. Id autem est principium vrbis, & quasi seminarium reip. Cic. Offic. lib.] 1. \[The first society is in marriage itself, etc. And this is the beginning of a city, and as it were the seedbed of the commonwealth.\]]

1\. That the world might be increased, and not merely increased, but with legitimate offspring and distinct families, which are the seedbeds of cities and commonwealths. Yes, and also that the church in the world might be preserved and propagated by a holy seed, Mal 2:15.

2\. That men might avoid fornication (1 Cor 7:2) and control their own bodies in holiness and honor. Because of that #footnote[#emph[Qui tentationum procellas cum difficultate salutis tolerant, coniugij portum petant. Greg. in Post. part.] 3. #emph[admon.] 28.] proneness to lust which is in man’s corrupt nature, this end adds much to the honor of marriage. It shows that marriage is like a haven for those whose salvation is in jeopardy through the gusts of temptation to lust. No sin is more hereditary; there is none in which more children of Adam share than this. Well might Christ say (Matt 19:11), “Not everyone can accept this word.”

Of all the children of Adam that ever were, not one in a million of those who have come to ripeness of years has been a true #emph[eunuch] all his lifetime. Against this hereditary disease no remedy is so sovereign as this. Yes, for those who do not have the gift of self-control, this is the only warranted and sanctified remedy.

3\. That man and wife might be a mutual help to one #footnote[#emph[Ad adiumentum mulier data est viro. Ambr. Offic. l.] 1. #emph[c.] 28. \[Woman was given to man as a help.\]] another (Gen 2:18): a help both in bringing forth and in bringing up children, and both in setting up and in well governing their family. A help also in well ordering prosperity and in well bearing adversity. A help in health and sickness. #footnote[#emph[Ut non solum honestè, sed & commodè vivat. Chrys. in Gen.] 2. #emph[hom.] 14. \[That he may live not only honorably but also in comfort.\]] A help while both live together, and when one is taken from the other by death. In this respect it is said (Prov 18:22), “He who finds a wife finds a good thing,” which by the rule of relation is also true of a husband. #footnote[Licet adiuvent hominem laboribus suis multa irrationabilia, nullum tamen par est rationali mulieri. Chrys. ibid.]

Man can have no such help from any other creature as from a wife, nor a woman as from a husband.

=== §. 25. #emph[Of the Privileges of marriage.]

#smallcaps[III]. If#footnote[Rom 3:1.] it is asked (as it once was of circumcision) what is the privilege, advantage and profit of marriage, I answer, “Much in every way.”

1\. By it men and women are made #emph[husbands] and #emph[wives.]

2\. It is the only lawful means to make them #emph[fathers] and #emph[mothers.]

3\. It is the ordinary means to make them #emph[masters] and #emph[mistresses.] All these are great dignities, in which the image and glory of God consists.

4\. It is the most effectual means of continuing a man’s #footnote[#emph[Unusquisque in liberis suis quodammodo renascitur etc. Plato dè Leg. lib.] 4. \[Everyone is in a manner born again in his children, etc.\]] name and memory in this world that there can be. Children are living monuments and vivid representations of their parents.

5\. In ancient times many privileges were granted to those who were married. #footnote[#emph[Appian. lib.] 2. #emph[debel. ciu.]] In pleading cases or giving sentence they had the first place, and in the choice of men for offices they were preferred. #footnote[Sueton. in Aug.] In assemblies they had the place of honor. #footnote[#emph[Arist. Polit. li.] 2. #emph[cap. 7. & Codic lib.] 10. #emph[tit.] 63.] And if they had many children they were exempt from night watches and other such burdensome duties. Among us, if the younger sister is married before the elder, pre-eminence and precedence are given to the younger.

The privileges and honors which are given to married persons were, without question, the ground of the custom of his country which Laban mentions:#footnote[Gen 29:26.] that the younger was not to be married before the elder.

=== §. 26. #emph[Of the mystery of Marriage.]

#smallcaps[IIII]. Great is the mystery which is set forth by marriage: namely, the sacred, spiritual, real and inviolable union between Christ and his church. It is excellently portrayed in Solomon’s Song and in Psalm 45, and expressly noted in Eph 5:32.

By this, man and wife who love one another wholeheartedly, as they ought, have an evident demonstration of Christ’s love to them. For as parents, by the affection which they bear their children, may better discern the mind and intent of God toward them than those who never had a child, so married persons may better know the disposition of Jesus Christ, who is the spouse of every faithful soul, than single persons.

=== §. 27. #emph[Of Marriage and single life compared together.]

Let the admirers and praisers of the single state now bring out all their reasons and put them in the other scale against marriage. If these two are duly balanced and rightly weighed, we will find single life too light to be compared with honorable marriage. All that can be said for the single state is grounded on incidental circumstances. Saint Paul, who of all the writers of holy Scripture has spoken most for it, brings all his commendations under the head of #emph[expediency,] and limits them all to the #footnote[1 Cor 7:26 διὰ τὴν ἐνεστῶσαν ἀνάγκην.] “present necessity.”

#emph[Objection.] He uses these words#footnote[#emph[v.] 1.] (#emph[good] and #emph[better]).#footnote[#emph[v.] 38.]

#emph[Answer.] Those words relate not to virtue but to expediency. Nor are they spoken in opposition to vice and sin, for then it would follow that to marry (which is God’s ordinance, and honorable in all) is evil and sinful. That is to revive #footnote[#emph[Saturniliani de quibus Epiph. haer.] 23.] that ancient heresy that “marriage is of the devil.” #footnote[Si quis coinquinationem vocet legitimam commixtionem, habet inhabitatorem draconem Apostatam. Ignat. Epist. ad Philad.] Of old, those who called lawful marriage a defilement have been said to have the apostate dragon dwelling in them. But the apostle calls that #emph[good] which is advantageous, and that #emph[better] which is more expedient; and yet not absolutely more expedient, but to some persons at some times. For if any do not have the gift of self-control, it is not only advantageous or more expedient that they marry, but also absolutely necessary. They are commanded to do so (1 Cor 7:9). Yet on the other side, if any have the gift of self-control, they are not absolutely barred from marriage; there are other occasions, besides avoiding fornication, to move them to marry. It is therefore truly said that “virginity is not commanded, but advised.” #footnote[#emph[Virginitas non est iussa sed admonita. Bernard. de modo benè viu. serm.] 21.] “We have no precept for it, but leave it to the power of those who have that power.” So far as men and women see just occasion for abstaining from marriage (being at least able to do so), they are persuaded by the apostle to use their liberty and keep themselves free. But all the occasions which #footnote[#emph[De virginitate praeceptum non habemus: volentium potestati id relinquimus. Constit. Apost. li.] 4. #emph[cap.] 14.] move them to remain single arise from the weakness and wickedness of men: the wickedness of those who stir up troubles against others, and the weakness of those who allow themselves to be disquieted and too much distracted with the affairs of the family, care for wife, children and the like.#footnote[1 Cor 7:32, 33, 34.] Were it not for the wickedness of some and the weakness of others, #emph[to please a husband or a wife] would be no hindrance #emph[to pleasing the Lord.] If, therefore, man had stood in his whole and innocent state, no such wickedness or weakness would have seized on him, and then the single state could in no respect have been preferred #footnote[#emph[S. Ecclesia & virginitatem glorificat, & nuptias pudicas honorat. Epiph. haer.] 48.] before the married. But since the fall, virginity (where it is given) may be of good use; and therefore the church gives due honor both to virginity and to marriage.

=== §. 28. #emph[Of celebrating marriage with sorrow.]

Contrary to the joyful celebrating of marriage described above are all those crooked courses which bring much grief, trouble and vexation to it: such as forced, stolen, unequal or in any other way unlawful marriages; marriages without the consent of parents or other governors and friends; or marriages rushed through to avoid the danger of the law for impurity committed before, and the like. Many, by their disorderly and improper performing of so weighty a matter, not only cause great trouble and disquiet on the wedding day, but also much sorrow all the days of their life. If such people find no joy, comfort or help in marriage, but rather the contrary, let them not blame God’s ordinance, but their own folly and perverseness.
